## Why This Is Asked
Tests core RN data flow patterns: FlatList rendering with performance optimization (keyExtractor, onEndReachedThreshold), custom hooks for separation of concerns, pagination logic, error boundary handling, and practical offline persistence—essential skills for production mobile apps.

## Key Concepts
- **FlatList optimization**: keyExtractor for stable identity, onEndReachedThreshold for scroll detection, removeClippedSubviews for memory
- **Custom hooks**: encapsulate fetching logic, return stable API for consumers, handle loading/error states
- **Pagination**: page-based or cursor-based, track hasMore state, prevent duplicate requests
- **Offline caching**: AsyncStorage.getItem on mount, setItem on successful fetch, handle stale data gracefully
- **Error handling**: try/catch with user-friendly messages, retry mechanisms, loading indicators

## Data Model
```typescript
interface Bike {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  latitude: number;
  longitude: number;
  distance: number; // meters from user
  available: boolean;
  type: 'standard' | 'electric' | 'cargo';
  pricePerMinute: number;
}
```

## Code Example
```javascript
// useNearbyBikes.ts
export function useNearbyBikes() {
  const [bikes, setBikes] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);
  const [refreshing, setRefreshing] = useState(false);

  const STORAGE_KEY = '@nearby_bikes';

  const loadCached = async () => {
    const cached = await AsyncStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    if (cached) setBikes(JSON.parse(cached));
  };

  const fetchBikes = async (pageNum = 1, isRefresh = false) => {
    try {
      if (isRefresh) setRefreshing(true);
      else if (pageNum === 1) setLoading(true);

      const res = await fetch(`/api/bikes?page=${pageNum}&limit=20`);
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch bikes');
      const data = await res.json();

      setBikes(prev => pageNum === 1 ? data.bikes : [...prev, ...data.bikes]);
      setHasMore(data.hasMore);
      setPage(pageNum);

      await AsyncStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(
        pageNum === 1 ? data.bikes : [...(pageNum === 1 ? [] : bikes), ...data.bikes]
      ));
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message || 'Something went wrong');
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
      setRefreshing(false);
    }
  };

  useEffect(() => { loadCached(); fetchBikes(1); }, []);

  return {
    bikes,
    loading: loading && bikes.length === 0,
    error,
    refresh: () => fetchBikes(1, true),
    loadMore: () => { if (hasMore && !loading) fetchBikes(page + 1); },
  };
}

// BikeListScreen.tsx
function BikeListScreen() {
  const { bikes, loading, error, refresh, loadMore } = useNearbyBikes();

  if (error && bikes.length === 0) return <ErrorView message={error} onRetry={refresh} />;

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={bikes}
      keyExtractor={(item) => item.id}
      renderItem={({ item }) => <BikeCard bike={item} />}
      onRefresh={refresh}
      refreshing={false}
      onEndReached={loadMore}
      onEndReachedThreshold={0.5}
      ListFooterComponent={loading ? <ActivityIndicator /> : null}
    />
  );
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- Forgetting onEndReachedThreshold causes loadMore to fire too early or never
- Not tracking hasMore results in duplicate API calls at list end
- AsyncStorage is async—never assume immediate availability
- Missing error state causes silent failures visible only in console
- Not using keyExtractor forces FlatList to use index, causing render bugs on prepend

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-thread data flow, Native Modules evolution (TurboModules, Fabric), and performance at scale in RN.

## Key Concepts
- TurboModule + Fabric bridge vs legacy bridge
- worker threads, WebSocket decoding
- ring buffer + JSI bridge; minimal GC pressure
- memoized React rendering with immutable price maps

## Code Example
```javascript
function useLivePrices(symbols) {
  const [prices, setPrices] = useState(new Map());
  useEffect(() => {
    const sub = NativeLivePrices.subscribe(symbols, (updates) => {
      setPrices(prev => new Map([...updates]));
    });
    return () => sub.unsubscribe();
  }, [symbols]);
  return prices;
}
```

